WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.579 --> 00:00:20.989
Why don't we start, Jai? Do you want to start? Each of you

00:00:20.989 --> 00:00:23.779
give you a little brief intro overview of your journey and

00:00:23.799 --> 00:00:26.609
entrepreneurship, and then we will launch into this very

00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:29.849
pressing - oh, yes, please, our cell phones need to be off.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.739
We are taping so cell phones interfere with the

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.259
transmission. So if you can please turn your phones off,

00:00:35.259 --> 00:00:38.399
that would be hugely helpful. So thank you. I have Nancy

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.599
here to keep me honest. Jai, you want to start?

00:00:42.619 --> 00:00:45.619
Sure. My name is Jai Prakash graduate from the GSB last year

00:00:45.619 --> 00:00:49.069
in June. And so my journey to entrepreneurship, I started -

00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:52.579
prior to the GSB, I spent a few years in consulting in

00:00:52.599 --> 00:00:56.069
sports and a couple years of private equity. And so I came

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.589
to the GSB thinking I want to do something more

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:01.089
entrepreneurial. And I used my summer between my first and

00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:04.090
second year to work at - I started to call Bonobos which was

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.090
started by some GSB of '07. You guys have probably seen e-

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.629
mails about it by now. I really enjoyed that, came back my

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:13.900
second year deciding I wanted to do something that was very

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.070
entrepreneurial and focusing on its products, spent a lot of

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.090
company meeting with different companies that came with

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.089
different ideas of my own, and ended up at a company called

00:01:22.089 --> 00:01:25.920
reBloom which right now, I am the COO, I was employee #2.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:28.069
The product launched in May, and we sell it two and half

00:01:28.069 --> 00:01:30.479
ounce natural sleep drink that's an alternative to things

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:31.479
like Ambien

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:35.950
[inaudible] and so. I started in July, and I have been there

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:36.969
for nine months.

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:42.700
So my name is Ilana Stern. I am the founder and CEO of

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:46.079
WeddingtonWay which is an online boutique for bridesmaid

00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:49.090
dresses. It's basically essential shopping for bridal

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:54.329
parties. I was a buyer for Bloomingdale's prior to the GSB

00:01:54.349 --> 00:01:58.849
and came in sort of knowing that I was passionate about the

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:01.310
fashion industry and fascinated by a lot of the

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:03.859
inefficiencies in the industry but not sure yet what I

00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:08.089
wanted to do about it. Basically, I started working on the

00:02:08.109 --> 00:02:11.219
idea for WeddingtonWay in the summer between my first and

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:16.499
second year. A lot of actually time spent with Jai, we

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:19.799
shared an office at Bonobos for a couple of weeks during the

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:23.079
summer. And I actually did it something different than Jai,

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:25.889
which is spent part of the summer in consulting and part of

00:02:25.889 --> 00:02:28.889
the summer at Bonobos. Half of my time at Bonobos was spent

00:02:28.889 --> 00:02:31.889
doing project for them and half of it was spent doing a

00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:35.689
project for myself. And basically, it just got going in the

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.879
second year with WeddingtonWay, took advantage of the time

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.659
at the GSB to do a massive amount of consumer research. It

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:45.539
was really the most important thing for us to understand

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:48.789
where the pain was coming from with brides in the process,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.009
and incorporated WeddingtonWay in May of my second year, got

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.749
our first commitments from investors the week of graduation

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:01.159
and started full time really the day after graduation.

00:03:01.179 --> 00:03:05.739
So my name is Alex Salazar. I am a GSB-09 my company is

00:03:05.759 --> 00:03:09.799
called Katasoft, and we are making application security

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:12.449
software. So we are trying to make application security

00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:15.999
easy. And our projects base on open source project that we

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:19.019
also have called Apache Shiro. So we are very much in the

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:21.109
enterprise software space. My background prior to the GSB

00:03:21.109 --> 00:03:24.039
was enterprise sales for IBM, so I have always lived in

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:30.309
enterprise software. While at the GSB, I was very busy. I

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:33.729
ran the E-Club along with Daniel who is not here right now,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.519
and I also put together the E-Conference. And it was a great

00:03:38.539 --> 00:03:41.039
time. My summer interest was in venture capital, also folks

00:03:41.039 --> 00:03:44.019
in enterprise software. And then I spent my second year

00:03:44.039 --> 00:03:47.319
flushing out of thesis around Cloud software, tried to start

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.859
a company based off that. That company wasn't not working

00:03:49.859 --> 00:03:52.609
out, and then a few months later, ended up finding this

00:03:52.609 --> 00:03:54.609
opportunity and started this company with a different

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:57.609
partner, which took about nine months after graduating.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:00.679
The first prescribed question we have is about what you

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:03.109
didn't take advantage of the business school. But these

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:07.419
three student alums I think were particularly good about

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:09.799
taking advantage of many things at the business school. So

00:04:09.799 --> 00:04:12.549
in addition to talking about what you wish you take an

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:15.049
advantage of, how much you guys in particular just talk

00:04:15.049 --> 00:04:17.550
about what you didn't take advantage of because they were

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.389
really full-time residents in the CES. So one or the other

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:24.590
way, Alex, maybe you talked about it a little bit.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.860
So I took advantage of everything I could get my hands on.

00:04:27.879 --> 00:04:33.139
The CES was ground zero. I spent I think at least twice two

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:41.340
days a week just at the CES with Lynda, with Nancy and just

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.159
using that as kind of my headquarters for all the work I was

00:04:44.159 --> 00:04:47.740
doing. I also really leveraged the network that the school

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:50.759
has. I mean when you are a student, you have access to

00:04:50.759 --> 00:04:54.090
everyone. Anyone and everyone will take your call; anyone

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.279
and everyone will give you time; anyone and everyone will

00:04:56.279 --> 00:04:59.970
tell you information they shouldn't give you. And so I took

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.949
advantage of that fantastic network of people that have

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.139
really, really helped and some of which have financed our

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:09.389
company. And I also participated in everything the

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:12.060
Entrepreneurship Club had which also gave me a very strong

00:05:12.079 --> 00:05:14.430
network of fellow classmates and alums who were also

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:17.220
entrepreneurs, which has been probably one of the most

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.990
important things in the first year in just helping to stay

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.709
same, is having people around and going the same --

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:24.729
Is there anything that you think you missed?

00:05:24.729 --> 00:05:27.409
Yes. So the flipside to that equation was that I had no

00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:31.110
spare time while I was in school, and that actually from an

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:33.780
entrepreneurship standpoint was difficult because I didn't

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.139
have a lot of free time to really evaluate ideas in the

00:05:39.159 --> 00:05:41.909
capacity that I should have been evaluating them when I was

00:05:41.909 --> 00:05:44.659
in school, and I didn't have the opportunity to really just

00:05:44.659 --> 00:05:47.159
spend time working on concept and testing them while a

00:05:47.159 --> 00:05:49.719
student. I had to actually wait until I graduated and then

00:05:49.719 --> 00:05:53.360
had spare time to go do that work. In hindsight, it would

00:05:53.379 --> 00:05:54.379
have

00:05:54.379 --> 00:05:58.019
[inaudible] with the working wife, yeah.

00:05:58.039 --> 00:06:01.899
So I think we are probably all going to echo each other to a

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:06.399
certain perspective. But essentially, for me, I think the

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:09.089
most beneficial thing was really getting out there, talking

00:06:09.109 --> 00:06:12.280
to alums, talking to people connected with community,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.860
investors, other entrepreneurs and just getting a lot of

00:06:15.879 --> 00:06:20.160
feedback on the idea as it was evolving. And there was sort

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.230
of nothing more beneficial from just a testing the idea

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.629
perspective, from the building confidence perspective,

00:06:25.649 --> 00:06:28.589
learning how to talk to other entrepreneurs, learning how to

00:06:28.609 --> 00:06:32.060
talk to investors and really sort of getting hang of how

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:33.639
everything works.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.549
Can you talk about how you either did or did not do that in

00:06:36.549 --> 00:06:37.919
the context of class as well?

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:42.199
Yeah. So okay, so that's a good point. So I would say what I

00:06:42.219 --> 00:06:45.089
wish I had done more of actually is taking more

00:06:45.109 --> 00:06:50.959
entrepreneurial classes earlier. Our first year was a little

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:54.180
bit less flexible in terms of extra classes we could take

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:59.399
[inaudible]. But I did get a massive amount out of it. So I

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:03.500
took 356 in my second year, and we developed a lot of what

00:07:03.519 --> 00:07:07.379
WeddingtonWay is through the 356 class. That was huge, just

00:07:07.399 --> 00:07:10.439
getting other students involved in the idea, seeing like

00:07:10.459 --> 00:07:14.009
learning how to excite a team, how to pitch every other

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:17.730
week. It was just incredibly valuable, formation of new

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:23.389
ventures, MGE with Joel Peterson, another phenomenal class

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:27.639
and just a lot of the classes where it's just sort of throw

00:07:27.659 --> 00:07:31.769
away the cases and really dig into a real business idea, do

00:07:31.789 --> 00:07:34.660
role playing where you are really sort of stepping outside

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:38.389
your comfort zone. I think a lot of entrepreneurship is

00:07:38.409 --> 00:07:42.439
doing things you don't know how to do every single day. And

00:07:42.459 --> 00:07:46.259
you have - a lot of the entrepreneurial classes really

00:07:46.279 --> 00:07:49.589
helped me sort of step outside of my comfort zone and keep

00:07:49.609 --> 00:07:52.609
pushing myself to the next level, so that I really sort of

00:07:52.609 --> 00:07:55.250
became conditioned and got totally used to it.

00:07:55.269 --> 00:07:57.139
So what did you miss or what you wish you had done?

00:07:57.139 --> 00:08:00.790
Yeah. So I mean just I think getting sort of - I think the

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.739
biggest thing I didn't do was there was so much benefit in

00:08:03.739 --> 00:08:07.069
reaching out to the '09 network in my second year, the

00:08:07.089 --> 00:08:11.240
network around Stanford. And I wish I had done more pf that

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:14.509
my first year. So I guess this is a little bit more relevant

00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:17.389
for first years. Just started finding excuses to reach out

00:08:17.409 --> 00:08:22.159
to alums as early as you can and just start figuring out how

00:08:22.179 --> 00:08:23.679
to engage people in conversation. It's really beneficial

00:08:23.679 --> 00:08:24.679
early on.

00:08:24.679 --> 00:08:25.679
Jai.

00:08:25.679 --> 00:08:33.639
Yeah. So things I did that were good, I think so I am

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:37.179
talking about classes, the three classes that I took that

00:08:37.199 --> 00:08:39.949
are probably the most valuable for me right now are MGE,

00:08:39.949 --> 00:08:43.559
well I think it goes back - I think when I took it, I

00:08:43.579 --> 00:08:46.579
thought it was like okay, when I am running a company 10

00:08:46.579 --> 00:08:49.579
years from now, I will use these things. But the lessons you

00:08:49.579 --> 00:08:51.829
learn, they come up in everyday conversations with vendors,

00:08:51.829 --> 00:08:54.329
with hiring, with whatever. And so it's been super valuable.

00:08:54.329 --> 00:08:57.259
Touch-feely is an amazing class, so I think everyone should

00:08:57.279 --> 00:09:00.279
try and take it your first year here. You still have the

00:09:00.279 --> 00:09:03.529
chance to sign up. And then 356 I took which was just a

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:06.779
startup class and so I didn't have an idea that I had - I

00:09:06.779 --> 00:09:09.529
wanted brand new, I wanted the consumer products, and so I

00:09:09.529 --> 00:09:12.529
worked with another guy, I had a sneaker idea. And just I

00:09:12.529 --> 00:09:15.529
was able to use that to leverage how I think about selling,

00:09:15.529 --> 00:09:18.779
how I think about customers, who I would reach out to in the

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:21.529
GSB network. Outside of that, the only thing I really did

00:09:21.529 --> 00:09:24.279
was my second year, I started the speaker series through the

00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:26.529
CES which was called the Consumer Product Founders Series,

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:29.029
and it was probably more selfish than anything because I

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:32.029
knew I wanted to be in consumer products, and I just needed

00:09:32.029 --> 00:09:34.529
to reach out to as many people from the GSB

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:37.279
[inaudible] brought them in. I think we had to bring in like

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:40.779
10 or 11 speakers in over a few months. So that was a really

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:44.029
useful thing for me. I am still in touch with a handful of

00:09:44.029 --> 00:09:46.779
them, and they have been advisors for me. I don't have

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:50.029
really any regrets from the GSB. I think I did a lot of

00:09:50.029 --> 00:09:53.029
things I wanted to do. And it's one of those unique times

00:09:53.029 --> 00:09:56.029
where you can kind of learn whatever you want to learn and

00:09:56.029 --> 00:09:58.279
[inaudible] time whenever you want to. I did two things

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:00.529
differently. One would be potentially just putting a stake

00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:03.029
[inaudible] earlier what I wanted to do. I kind of came

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:05.279
back. I came first year at the GSB not

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:07.779
[inaudible] the start up, go back to private equity, go back

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:10.279
to sports, do whatever. But then second year, I started

00:10:10.279 --> 00:10:12.779
realizing I want to be in consumer products and so I started

00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:13.779
to open

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:15.779
[inaudible]. And it was much easier to have conversations

00:10:15.779 --> 00:10:18.029
with alums, with fellow classmates, whatever in terms of

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:21.279
saying this is where I want to go. And the other thing which

00:10:21.279 --> 00:10:24.529
has nothing to do with startup or anything is like I wish I

00:10:24.529 --> 00:10:26.779
would have just played more tennis, played more basketball,

00:10:26.779 --> 00:10:29.779
hung out more because the second you get out, the second you

00:10:29.779 --> 00:10:33.029
think of a startup, there is so much to do and so little

00:10:33.029 --> 00:10:35.529
time for anything else. So take advantage of being there.

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:39.029
I think Jai had a great point that I mean he said kind of

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:41.029
jokingly, he started the consumer products speakers series

00:10:41.029 --> 00:10:43.779
selfishly but - and it doesn't just hold to the GSB, it

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:46.529
holds I think in general in the population. If you do

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:49.029
something to kind of serve what your personal desires and

00:10:49.029 --> 00:10:51.779
needs are, you are going to serve a broader population as

00:10:51.779 --> 00:10:55.029
well. And he gives you an excuse to reach out to people and

00:10:55.029 --> 00:10:57.279
build those deep relationships to become a great foundation

00:10:57.279 --> 00:11:00.279
for other things as well. And it doesn't just hold here at

00:11:00.279 --> 00:11:03.279
the Business School, I think it holds in life in general. So

00:11:03.279 --> 00:11:06.939
what have been your biggest lessons achieved, learned up to

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:10.879
this point? Ilana, you are in the middle --

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:19.549
I would say there are a lot of different ways I could answer

00:11:19.569 --> 00:11:23.850
that because I learned lessons every day. But some of the

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:31.679
takeaways I have so far, #1, follow your guide. For me, in

00:11:31.699 --> 00:11:35.710
everything I do, there is a point at which you just can't

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:39.179
rationalize a decision anymore and you just have to sort of

00:11:39.199 --> 00:11:43.740
let your instinct guide you in terms of whether it's forming

00:11:43.759 --> 00:11:49.179
your team or building relationships with like suppliers or

00:11:49.199 --> 00:11:54.509
partners, just stuff I do every day. It's really important

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:56.829
to follow my guide because ultimately we are moving pretty

00:11:56.829 --> 00:12:00.509
quickly, and I sort of have to just make sure I continue to

00:12:00.529 --> 00:12:04.759
push the business forward. And other thing that has been

00:12:04.779 --> 00:12:08.899
incredibly important is transparency, and this goes back to

00:12:08.919 --> 00:12:12.529
Jai's point with touchy-feely. A lot of what I learned in

00:12:12.549 --> 00:12:17.019
touchy-feely, I do use every day. Learning, just being

00:12:17.039 --> 00:12:21.330
really open and honest and communicative with everyone that

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:24.600
I work with and just really laying it out on the table has

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.590
been absolutely paramount to just sort of building a strong

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:31.559
team and accomplishing what we need to accomplish. So I

00:12:31.579 --> 00:12:34.579
would say those are sort of the two things that drive me

00:12:34.579 --> 00:12:35.929
every day.

00:12:35.949 --> 00:12:38.899
I agree with that stuff. I really thought about this and

00:12:38.919 --> 00:12:41.480
there are four things that I think a key lesson I learned.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.870
One is my situation is actually a little different than

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:47.519
these two in the sense that there was a founder who had

00:12:47.519 --> 00:12:50.769
developed a product over a year and a half and then he was

00:12:50.769 --> 00:12:54.019
bringing me on as his first hire. But in the sense of you

00:12:54.019 --> 00:12:56.519
thinking about doing a startup with a cofounder or whatever,

00:12:56.519 --> 00:12:59.019
one thing I was told before going into entrepreneurship was

00:12:59.019 --> 00:13:02.299
that it's like a marriage and I didn't really believe that,

00:13:02.319 --> 00:13:04.949
but the reality is this that John, the founder of

00:13:04.969 --> 00:13:06.969
[inaudible], spends more time with me than his wife

00:13:06.969 --> 00:13:09.969
probably, and we get lunch every day together, we are in the

00:13:09.969 --> 00:13:12.719
office, get every day together. And so one of the biggest

00:13:12.719 --> 00:13:14.969
lessons I learned is that underlying values are critical.

00:13:14.969 --> 00:13:18.469
Like as long as you guys - we see the same way in terms of

00:13:18.469 --> 00:13:21.469
how we want to do business, how we want to build the

00:13:21.469 --> 00:13:23.969
company, that's really important. And we are in the process

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:26.719
of recruiting. Right now, we would bring on a third employee

00:13:26.719 --> 00:13:29.219
today actually and hopefully a fourth one the next month.

00:13:29.219 --> 00:13:32.219
And like as we have gone through the process of meeting a

00:13:32.219 --> 00:13:34.969
lot of people, it's been very critical for us to decide

00:13:34.969 --> 00:13:37.719
whether that person fits our values and wants to grow the

00:13:37.719 --> 00:13:40.969
business in the way we do. And there is a lot of situations

00:13:40.969 --> 00:13:43.719
where your values are tested. So I think that's the biggest

00:13:43.719 --> 00:13:46.469
lesson I have taken away. Second is that startups are super

00:13:46.469 --> 00:13:49.219
volatile, so you better be ready for ups and downs every

00:13:49.219 --> 00:13:52.719
day. I mean the way I would like to say is it's ups and

00:13:52.719 --> 00:13:55.719
downs but always moving forward. I mean there is a punch in

00:13:55.719 --> 00:13:58.469
the stomach every day for random reason. You have just got

00:13:58.469 --> 00:14:01.719
to be able to handle it and move forward and realize that if

00:14:01.719 --> 00:14:04.969
you have a product you like or a company you believe in that

00:14:04.969 --> 00:14:08.219
you are going to move forward and pick out the right to get

00:14:08.219 --> 00:14:10.969
things done. Another thing is that if you are doing a

00:14:10.969 --> 00:14:13.969
startup, you should try and find a way to have balance in

00:14:13.969 --> 00:14:17.219
it. I mean you are taking a giant pay-cut and you are taking

00:14:17.219 --> 00:14:19.469
the ability to control your schedule. So realize that

00:14:19.469 --> 00:14:22.219
nothing is really that urgent, and you should be able to

00:14:22.219 --> 00:14:23.969
find a balance enjoying what you are doing feeling happy,

00:14:23.969 --> 00:14:24.969
like

00:14:24.969 --> 00:14:26.719
[inaudible] also having a balance with non-startup like

00:14:26.719 --> 00:14:28.219
things. And then it was already

00:14:28.219 --> 00:14:30.219
[inaudible] things just that the GSB network is incredible.

00:14:30.219 --> 00:14:32.969
Actually, I came in through reBloom right when we were about

00:14:32.969 --> 00:14:35.719
to launch our fundraising process. And I will tell you that

00:14:35.719 --> 00:14:38.579
any GSB alumni e-mail whether they were at a C fund or

00:14:38.579 --> 00:14:40.259
institutional fund, individual, angel or whatever, every

00:14:40.259 --> 00:14:41.870
single one of them applied to

00:14:41.889 --> 00:14:42.889
[inaudible]. And even once

00:14:42.889 --> 00:14:45.319
[inaudible] want to take a meeting and nothing to do with

00:14:45.319 --> 00:14:47.569
consumer products were responsive and helpful. And if we

00:14:47.569 --> 00:14:50.069
look at the people that have eventually invested, I would

00:14:50.069 --> 00:14:53.069
say - I mean the large majority of them are GSB alums or

00:14:53.069 --> 00:14:55.819
type so GSB somehow. So I think the network is amazing --

00:14:55.819 --> 00:15:02.539
So for me, the biggest lesson I have learned over the first

00:15:02.559 --> 00:15:04.860
year is that customer feedback or feedback from the outside

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:10.519
world is tricky. It's like dynamite. You can't create a

00:15:10.539 --> 00:15:13.720
company without it, you can't a product without it. But if

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.689
you use it the wrong way, you will blow your hands off. So

00:15:17.709 --> 00:15:21.490
part of that lesson was being really clear on what feedback

00:15:21.509 --> 00:15:24.569
you actually want from the people you meet with because

00:15:24.589 --> 00:15:29.419
everyone will give you feedback. And so an example of that

00:15:29.439 --> 00:15:32.679
is never ask someone what they think of your product, don't

00:15:32.699 --> 00:15:36.149
ask them that question. The question that matters is will

00:15:36.169 --> 00:15:39.289
they buy it. And if they buy it, how will they use it,

00:15:39.289 --> 00:15:42.159
because if you ask them what they think about it, they may

00:15:42.159 --> 00:15:45.089
tell you all the things that are wrong with it, all the

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:47.839
things that they think are awesome about it, and none of

00:15:47.839 --> 00:15:51.089
those may have any impact on whether or not they buy it. And

00:15:51.089 --> 00:15:53.839
so somebody may tell you because you are trying to be

00:15:53.839 --> 00:15:56.909
helpful that oh, here are all the critics I have for your

00:15:56.929 --> 00:15:59.679
product, and they really are doing from a good place. And

00:15:59.679 --> 00:16:02.179
you come out of that meeting thinking your product sucks,

00:16:02.179 --> 00:16:05.179
they don't want it. But then if you ask that same person,

00:16:05.179 --> 00:16:08.179
well would you but it based on what it currently is, they

00:16:08.179 --> 00:16:11.339
might say yes, and you need to know that. And so once we

00:16:11.339 --> 00:16:13.839
figured that out, we just stopped asking people what they

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:16.839
thought of our product and just the cut the question to will

00:16:16.839 --> 00:16:20.089
you buy it. The other thing is you need to be careful who

00:16:20.089 --> 00:16:23.339
you ask for feedback from. So the benefit of the GSB is you

00:16:23.339 --> 00:16:26.949
have an amazing network to talk to. That's really tricky

00:16:26.969 --> 00:16:29.449
because you might go meet with someone who is

00:16:29.469 --> 00:16:32.199
extraordinarily powerful and you go meet with them, and you

00:16:32.219 --> 00:16:35.870
get their feedback on your product. And that person tells

00:16:35.889 --> 00:16:38.529
you that it doesn't make any sense, they don't think it's

00:16:38.529 --> 00:16:41.419
going to have a good market, they don't see how it's going

00:16:41.419 --> 00:16:44.169
to go to market, it's going to be too expensive, integration

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:47.169
is going to be an issue. But when you actually start to

00:16:47.169 --> 00:16:49.919
think about it, they are not your customer, they have never

00:16:49.919 --> 00:16:52.669
been in your space, they have never done anything related to

00:16:52.669 --> 00:16:55.419
what you are starting, and so the feedback ended up being

00:16:55.419 --> 00:16:57.919
completely useless. Or vise versa, they may tell you it's

00:16:57.919 --> 00:17:00.419
completely awesome but all those things still hold so you

00:17:00.419 --> 00:17:02.669
get a false/negative or false/positive. So when you meet

00:17:02.669 --> 00:17:05.419
with people, you need to be really clear on what their

00:17:05.419 --> 00:17:07.730
feedback actually should matter to you. So if it's someone

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:10.480
who is really important, ask them a question that they know

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:13.730
and have credibility in. So if they have been a VP of sales

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.730
at some big company, ask them a question about how they are

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:19.480
going to market. Don't ask them about if your product is

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:22.480
going to sell because they are not going to know. If it's

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.230
Mark Zuckerberg, don't ask him if he likes your product. Ask

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.730
him how he would integrate to his sales platform or

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.980
something like that. So those things were very great

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:32.480
lessons. But now that we have learned them, our feedback

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:35.059
sessions and our customer meetings have become a lot more

00:17:35.079 --> 00:17:36.079
productive.

00:17:36.079 --> 00:17:37.079
[Informal Talk]

00:17:36.079 --> 00:17:41.410
Is there anything that you thought was particularly

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:45.539
important that ended up not being so?

00:17:45.559 --> 00:17:46.559
[Informal Talk]

00:17:46.559 --> 00:17:56.920
I don't know, I mean I am trying to say because I think I am

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.940
still learning every day. And for me, to tell you that some

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.690
class I took was important, next week it might become super

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:05.440
applicable. So for right now, I mean it's one of those

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:07.940
things where I am moving forward eyes wide open, always

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:10.440
learning. I mean I am finding that certain classes had

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.690
become applicable for certain things like I mentioned MGE

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:15.910
touchy-feely or sort of everyday things. But we have to deal

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.680
with negotiations, we are taking negotiations, that's

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.720
helpful. And I have no doubt that some of the accounting

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:21.740
stuff is --

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.740
I mean what about even things in your business? I mean did

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:26.990
you think raising money at a particular time was

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:32.190
particularly important or getting a particular customer?

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:36.149
I mean raising money was important I think. For us, we have

00:18:36.169 --> 00:18:38.519
tried to stay pretty balanced with how we approach things

00:18:38.519 --> 00:18:43.589
and don't let things affect us too much. So fundraising - we

00:18:43.609 --> 00:18:46.690
got done in December, we would like to get done in October/

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:49.929
November but it didn't hold us back. As long as you have

00:18:49.929 --> 00:18:52.579
cash in the bank and you feel like you can move forward,

00:18:52.579 --> 00:18:55.409
nothing is really stopping us. And so we have had delays and

00:18:55.409 --> 00:18:57.909
issues but I don't think there is anything that's surprised

00:18:57.909 --> 00:19:00.000
us too much.

00:19:00.019 --> 00:19:02.139
I think you guys put too much weight on --

00:19:02.139 --> 00:19:09.619
I think -- I don't know, I know sort of I would say through

00:19:09.639 --> 00:19:14.509
the GSB and through sort of training and everything like

00:19:14.529 --> 00:19:18.750
quant and finance, when you start a business, there can be a

00:19:18.769 --> 00:19:23.539
tendency to sort of want to model everything out to the last

00:19:23.559 --> 00:19:27.019
detail and make sure that you haven't missed anything. Two

00:19:27.039 --> 00:19:32.149
things I would say is understand your market opportunity,

00:19:32.169 --> 00:19:35.089
sort of understand your business model or want to think it's

00:19:35.089 --> 00:19:38.460
going to be. And you do want to make sure it's viable but

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.769
then move on because you could sort of spend all day in

00:19:42.789 --> 00:19:46.589
Excel or trying to make every assumption perfect, and the

00:19:46.609 --> 00:19:49.359
only way you can make an assumption perfect is to start

00:19:49.359 --> 00:19:52.109
running the business and have real data to plug into your

00:19:52.109 --> 00:19:58.680
model. It's I would say sort of one thing that was, I don't

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:01.950
know, that I have seen I guess a lot people sort of get

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:07.309
caught up on, I would say make sure not to.

00:20:07.329 --> 00:20:13.480
For me, everything I learned in the first year strategy

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:15.869
class. It's just isn't important in your first year

00:20:15.869 --> 00:20:19.960
strategy, like the best laid plans, you can think about

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:24.920
competitive advantage, and better entry, and supplier power,

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:28.450
and buyer power, and none of it matters because it's all

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:30.720
going to change dramatically when you are talking to

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.240
customers, and it's going to change every week until you

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:36.119
actually hone in on what your business model really is going

00:20:36.139 --> 00:20:40.029
to be. And I think what we came to was you don't define

00:20:40.049 --> 00:20:43.049
that. All that stuff that you learn in the strategy, you are

00:20:43.049 --> 00:20:45.779
evaluating these multibillion dollar companies, and you are

00:20:45.799 --> 00:20:48.849
analyzing their strategy. But you don't get to pick that

00:20:48.869 --> 00:20:51.869
really, I mean there is only small parts you get to pick.

00:20:51.869 --> 00:20:55.119
But a lot of is really reflection of the market you are in.

00:20:55.119 --> 00:20:57.869
It's a reflection of the space you are and the technology

00:20:57.869 --> 00:21:01.440
you have built. What I would say was most important in terms

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:05.740
of strategy is make sure you are in a big market and make

00:21:05.759 --> 00:21:08.480
sure you are in a market that's growing. And if you are in

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:11.230
those things, and you have a product people actually want to

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:14.799
buy because you are solving a real problem, everything else

00:21:14.819 --> 00:21:17.799
doesn't matter in the first year. It will matter if you

00:21:17.799 --> 00:21:21.779
become really big. But in the first year, the only thing

00:21:21.799 --> 00:21:25.509
matters is you have big market, you have growing market and

00:21:25.529 --> 00:21:28.920
you have a product people want to buy.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.049
So what have been some big inflection points for you in your

00:21:31.049 --> 00:21:32.049
businesses?

00:21:32.049 --> 00:21:33.049
[Informal Talk]

00:21:33.049 --> 00:21:54.319
So I think the first one for me was in November of my second

00:21:54.339 --> 00:21:59.849
year. I pitched WeddingtonWay the first time in a September

00:21:59.869 --> 00:22:03.519
session class and just started propelling forward into sort

00:22:03.539 --> 00:22:07.419
of working on the idea. In November I cold-called the bunch

00:22:07.439 --> 00:22:11.379
of brands in New York and basically said I am the founder of

00:22:11.399 --> 00:22:15.579
this like amazing company, and I am going to be in New York,

00:22:15.599 --> 00:22:19.559
will they meet with me. They somehow agreed. I got off the

00:22:19.579 --> 00:22:23.409
phone these seven brands like in one day and booked a ticket

00:22:23.429 --> 00:22:28.759
to New York, and the next week went to New York and met with

00:22:28.779 --> 00:22:32.240
all these brands, and got them to agree to sell to my non-

00:22:32.259 --> 00:22:37.039
existing company with a non-existing website. So that was

00:22:37.059 --> 00:22:41.529
really step one and that was really big because when our

00:22:41.549 --> 00:22:46.259
customer's inflection point #2 came to me in April saying

00:22:46.279 --> 00:22:48.960
basically they have heard that I was working on something

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.980
with bridesmaid dresses, can I help them, I was able to say

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.609
yes because I actually had product to sell to them even

00:22:54.609 --> 00:22:58.450
though I didn't have a website yet. I would say #3 was

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:02.680
probably being who is over there WeddingtonWay's director of

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:07.680
merchandising agreeing to join this crazy venture turning

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:11.440
one into two. So I was able to tell my investors that our

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:17.700
team size had doubled, which was really exciting. And then,

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:21.169
we launched the beta of our website in November. And from

00:23:21.189 --> 00:23:26.419
there, it's been moving at a million miles an hour, whether

00:23:26.439 --> 00:23:29.409
it's testing our first Facebook ads and realizing they are

00:23:29.429 --> 00:23:31.929
awesome for us, which is really exciting, or launching our

00:23:31.929 --> 00:23:34.250
first contest which we did on Monday with the wedding

00:23:34.250 --> 00:23:39.599
planning site. It's just sort of - I guess I would say that

00:23:39.619 --> 00:23:43.539
what is an inflection point that one of the pieces of advice

00:23:43.559 --> 00:23:46.059
have been given by a lot of different people, celebrate

00:23:46.059 --> 00:23:49.059
small victories at a startup because you have a lot of ups

00:23:49.059 --> 00:23:52.180
and down. So you can have inflection points every day.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:24:00.500
I mean with reBloom, some of the stuff that would be more

00:24:00.519 --> 00:24:03.019
interesting would be prior to when I started. Right now,

00:24:03.019 --> 00:24:05.519
there are a couple of inflection points when John was

00:24:05.519 --> 00:24:09.139
developing the product and there are a million. A couple of

00:24:09.159 --> 00:24:12.200
[inaudible] raised with friends and family around. So we had

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:14.439
some money in the bank and ended up agonizing over the

00:24:14.439 --> 00:24:18.490
decision to buy the URL Rebloom.com and spent $5,000

00:24:18.509 --> 00:24:22.710
[inaudible] because it just makes ton more sense to go into

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:25.490
business. And another thing, we spent about a third of this

00:24:25.509 --> 00:24:28.149
entire friends and family around on hiring a world-class

00:24:28.169 --> 00:24:31.799
packaging design company for the bottle and getting that all

00:24:31.819 --> 00:24:35.349
set up. And that was one thing I know he spent a ton of time

00:24:35.349 --> 00:24:40.369
debating but one piece of feedback we got from investors and

00:24:40.389 --> 00:24:43.079
potential advisors on it for ourselves is that the packaging

00:24:43.099 --> 00:24:45.599
looks great, and if you want to - in consumer products

00:24:45.599 --> 00:24:48.099
specifically, if you want to sell a premium product, you

00:24:48.099 --> 00:24:50.019
need to

00:24:50.039 --> 00:24:51.809
[inaudible] like something that targets to mass appeal.

00:24:51.809 --> 00:24:54.269
Inflection points as I joined, I mean the product launch

00:24:54.269 --> 00:24:57.269
about a month before I joined, and I would say there has

00:24:57.269 --> 00:25:00.519
been a bunch of stages in terms of - since I have started, I

00:25:00.519 --> 00:25:03.519
mean the big thing for me when I came in this fundraising.

00:25:03.519 --> 00:25:07.269
So obviously, that was my major focus coming in. And once we

00:25:07.289 --> 00:25:09.619
[inaudible] it's a big inflection point because we were

00:25:09.619 --> 00:25:12.549
talking about well, we have x amount in the bank, what are

00:25:12.549 --> 00:25:16.049
we going to do, what are we going to do. Then all of a

00:25:16.049 --> 00:25:19.549
sudden, we had a lot more in the bank, and it was like okay,

00:25:19.549 --> 00:25:22.049
now we can actually think about building this company. The

00:25:22.049 --> 00:25:24.299
next inflection point we are sort of still in right now is

00:25:24.299 --> 00:25:25.299
that

00:25:25.299 --> 00:25:27.549
[inaudible] to build the foundation, we get a new order

00:25:27.549 --> 00:25:29.799
management system, we are recruiting, we are building new

00:25:29.799 --> 00:25:32.299
office space, all that sort of stuff, things to put

00:25:32.299 --> 00:25:34.299
infrastructure in place that you can actually support

00:25:34.299 --> 00:25:37.549
growth. And I think we are getting to the point where we are

00:25:37.549 --> 00:25:40.299
reaching to the end of that inflection point and the next

00:25:40.299 --> 00:25:43.299
one is coming up now which is the idea of sales milestones.

00:25:43.299 --> 00:25:46.299
And actually, we have revenue but now it's like how do we

00:25:46.299 --> 00:25:49.299
take this business to the next level. So I think I mean -

00:25:49.299 --> 00:25:52.049
and that's over the course of nine months, we have been

00:25:52.049 --> 00:25:54.049
through a million different inflection points. There were

00:25:54.049 --> 00:25:57.299
three major ones that we have seen. So I mean one thing you

00:25:57.299 --> 00:25:59.799
will see with the startup is that the landscape shifts

00:25:59.799 --> 00:26:01.549
rapidly depending on how you are moving.

00:26:01.549 --> 00:26:04.299
We have had inflection points every day. Now, we have had

00:26:04.299 --> 00:26:06.549
two major inflection points. The first one was we

00:26:06.549 --> 00:26:10.569
bootstrapped for a long time because we wanted to be ready

00:26:10.589 --> 00:26:14.940
when we want to go, raise some money, and that was a bad

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.789
idea, because the inflection point for us was when we

00:26:18.809 --> 00:26:22.399
actually raised money, there is something psychological

00:26:22.419 --> 00:26:25.750
about actually having a salary and having other people's

00:26:25.769 --> 00:26:29.099
money to spend on the things you need to stand versus your

00:26:29.119 --> 00:26:32.589
own. And so the moment we had money in the bank, and there

00:26:32.609 --> 00:26:37.269
were enough zeros in the checking account, everybody --

00:26:37.289 --> 00:26:38.289
[Informal Talk]

00:26:38.289 --> 00:26:41.599
Everyone's felt a lot more comfortable, and our productivity

00:26:41.619 --> 00:26:44.869
skyrocketed. And it was just psychological because nothing

00:26:44.889 --> 00:26:47.619
else really changed, our burn rate didn't go up all that

00:26:47.619 --> 00:26:54.899
much. But there was a perceptible, perceivable shift in the

00:26:54.919 --> 00:26:58.799
company's mentality. So I would urge you to raise money. No

00:26:58.819 --> 00:27:01.189
matter what it is, raise some amount of money at the

00:27:01.189 --> 00:27:04.750
beginning so that you feel like you have a

00:27:04.769 --> 00:27:08.230
[inaudible]. The second inflection point for us was first

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:12.659
customer contact. We had designed this great concept on

00:27:12.679 --> 00:27:15.679
paper. We hadn't yet programmed anything but we had all the

00:27:15.679 --> 00:27:17.929
great PowerPoint slides that showed everything what we were

00:27:17.929 --> 00:27:20.679
going to do, and it was fantastic. And then we started

00:27:20.679 --> 00:27:23.179
showing it to customers. And that was an amazing experience

00:27:23.179 --> 00:27:25.909
because you think you know everything, you know nothing

00:27:25.929 --> 00:27:28.799
until customers start telling you about the real pains. And

00:27:28.819 --> 00:27:32.809
that feedback will - you cannot create a great company

00:27:32.829 --> 00:27:36.470
without it. So our company just from a product perspective

00:27:36.490 --> 00:27:39.049
took off once they started taking customer meetings. And we

00:27:39.069 --> 00:27:41.569
started doing it on a very, very regular basis, probably

00:27:41.569 --> 00:27:42.819
about three meetings a week.

00:27:42.069 --> 00:27:43.069
(Audience)

00:27:43.069 --> 00:28:08.559
So it's definitely I would say a 'work in progress,' and it'

00:28:08.579 --> 00:28:15.279
s probably the first question I have to answer every time I

00:28:15.299 --> 00:28:18.299
meet with an investor. So I guess I am probably used to

00:28:18.299 --> 00:28:21.549
talking about it. But it's been word of mouth for us, and we

00:28:21.549 --> 00:28:24.799
are starting to pay for it as well. But early on, our first

00:28:24.799 --> 00:28:27.599
two customers that came in April, one was a GSB classmate,

00:28:27.619 --> 00:28:31.579
